  "slug": "drug-antibiotic-024a",
  "summary": "Three recent preprints show that explicit structural assumptions in mechanistic models shape which predictions can be distinguished from one another.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is that inference from mechanistic models depends on explicit structural assumptions. In the receptor-ligand preprint, distinct catch-slip mechanisms produce identical multi-exponential survival forms, so survival data alone cannot discriminate them, and the authors propose CFPR as a network-level model of force-driven proofreading. The Piezo1 preprint couples channel conformation to membrane-cortex elasticity, yielding short-range attraction and long-range repulsion that produce finite clusters. The Dam1 study models the ring as a fuzzy disordered-protein complex, with microtubule-end structure governing multivalent kinetochore coupling. These systems are methodologically linked rather than mechanistically identical; what is new is that model-defining assumptions are treated as the object of analysis rather than background.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
